    "q-140839": {
      "dom_id": "step-q140839",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "If $C$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $\\lambda =5$ is an eigenvalue of $C,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $(C-5I)x=0$ has only zero solution\n\nII. $\\text{Col}(C-5I)\\ne {R}^{4}$\n\nIII. $\\text{Null}(C-5I)\\ne \\{0\\}$",
      "worked_solution": "Given a $4\\times 4$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=4=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nLet's now apply the theorem to the matrix $M=C-5I.$\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that if $\\lambda =5$ is an eigenvalue of $C,$ then the nonzero solutions of $(C-5I)x=0$ are eigenvectors of $C.$ As a result, the matrix $M=C-5I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statements II and III are true. Since $M=C-5I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{Col}(C-5I)\\ne {R}^{4},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(C-5I)\\ne \\{0\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
